  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nWhile the primary active zone of convection will be across the lower\nGreat Lakes today, there will be a weak disturbance south of there \nthat will move across the region during peak heating. The atmosphere\nwill become moderately unstable, but bulk shear will be on the \nweaker side. However, DCAPE will be around 1000 J/kg. So if storms \nbecome robust, then they will have the potential for strong\ndowndrafts that could lead to strong to damaging wind gusts. At this\nstage the greatest potential appears to be across southeast Indiana,\nsouthwest Ohio, and northern Kentucky. Precipitable water will be \nincreasing to around 1.8 inches, so storms could produce locally \nheavy rainfall, but they should be progressive enough to keep any \nhydrologic concerns at a minimum.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nActive zonal flow will remain across the region through the rest of\nthe weekend. Each wave of convection will be a little further south\nthan the previous one. So for tonight, expect primary activity to be\nacross northern Indiana and northern Ohio, but the southern flank of\nthis activity may move across northern counties. Parameters will be \nfavorable for efficient warm rain processes, and northern counties \nstill have the most vulnerable ground conditions due to previous \nheavy rainfall. It appears that activity will be progressive with a \nlimited potential for repeated rounds of rainfall, as may occur just \na bit further north. But this will have to be monitored closely in \ncase there are any southward adjustments to the more active \nconvective corridor.\n\nWeak low pressure and a cold front will move across the forecast \narea Sunday afternoon and evening. Another round of showers and\nsome thunderstorms will occur along and north of the front. There\nwill still be a heavy rain threat with precipitable water near 1.8\ninches and warm cloud depth around 4 km. Greatest potential will be\nany enhancement in precipitation intensity near the low pressure\nsystem.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3)\nIn the wake of the front, a cooler airmass will prevail over the\narea. A rather dynamic short wave moving out the Plains will close\noff in the Mississippi Valley and then slowly work its way east\nacross the region through mid week. This will result in periods of\nshowers and maybe some embedded thunderstorms.",
